    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing, what about edge cases? like when there's weird bugs or small glitches that make the screen reader skip things or the UI act up unexpectedly? how do i account for those in the audit?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a fantastic question, and honestly, the most important part of any audit is catching those sneaky bugs that only appear when things go wrong. You can't just test the happy path; you have to simulate the chaos.\n\nHere is how to account for those edge cases and glitches in your audit:\n\n**1. Simulate Network Failures and Timeouts**\n*   [ ] **The \"Goblin\" Test:** Intentionally disconnect your device or throttle the network speed to mimic a \"goblin\" blocking the connection. Does the screen reader announce that the page is loading, or does it get stuck in an infinite loop?\n*   [ ] **Retry Logic:** If a button fails to load, does the UI gracefully degrade, or does it crash the screen reader? A good app should announce \"Retry\" or \"Error\" clearly without confusing the user.\n\n**2. Test Rapid Interaction and Glitchy States**\n*   [ ] **The \"Goblin\" Tap:** Rapidly tap buttons, scroll, and swipe while a screen reader is active. Sometimes, rapid input causes the focus to jump to a weird element or the UI to freeze.\n*   [ ] **Focus Traps:**",
      "source": "target",
